  {"question": "According to arXiv 2510.21203, what lessons does the failure of the Baruch Plan provide for AI governance, and how do proposals like 'NPT+' adapt these lessons?", "answer": "The Baruch Plan's failure warns against proposals that lock in one nation's advantage; 'NPT+' adapts lessons by combining limited proliferation with benefit-sharing and norms.", "evidence": [{"page": 5, "quote": "Boudreaux et al. (2025) concludes the Soviet Union rejected the plan because it was perceived as an attempt to cement U.S. nuclear dominance (Boudreaux et al., 2025, pp. 6, 19). Extending lessons to AI, Boudreaux argues that proposals which lock in one nation's technological advantage cannot be expected to look fair or equitable to rivals (Boudreaux et al., 2025, p. 19)."}, {"page": 9, "quote": "One such model is Emery-Xu et al.'s 'NPT+', an adaptation of the NPT combining limited proliferation of advanced AI with norms of use and mechanisms for benefit-sharing (Emery-Xu et al., 2024, p. 16)."}]},
  {"question": "In arXiv 2510.21203, how do scholars use the concept of 'Mutual Assured AI Malfunction' (MAIM) and what role does deterrence play in AI governance proposals?", "answer": "MAIM adapts nuclear deterrence logic to AI, proposing that mutual vulnerability to AI infrastructure attacks could deter unilateral dominance attempts.", "evidence": [{"page": 6, "quote": "Looking towards a potential future with ASI, Hendrycks et al. (2025) adapt the logic of Mutual Assured Destruction (MAD) to propose 'Mutual Assured AI Malfunction' (MAIM). Under MAIM, mutual vulnerability to attacks targeting critical AI infrastructure could potentially deter unilateral attempts to achieve ASI dominance."} ]},
  {"question": "What does arXiv 2510.21203 define as 'Frontier AI', and how is this term used in policy proposals?", "answer": "'Frontier AI' refers to highly capable foundation models with dangerous capabilities sufficient to pose severe risk to public safety.", "evidence": [{"page": 7, "quote": "The term 'Frontier AI' refers to highly capable foundation models, which could have dangerous capabilities sufficient to pose severe risk to public safety (Anderljung et al., 2023, p. 7)."}]},
  {"question": "According to arXiv 2510.21203, what are the main critiques of using the IAEA as a model for international AI governance?", "answer": "Critiques include the IAEA's enforcement challenges, AI's intangible and fast-evolving nature, difficulty monitoring software, and lack of international consensus on AI risks.", "evidence": [{"page": 8, "quote": "Common critiques emphasize the IAEA's own historical enforcement challenges (A. Wasil, 2024), the difficulty of monitoring and controlling diffuse, rapidly evolving software compared to tangible nuclear materials (Cha, 2024, p. 12; Chesterman, 2021, p. 202; Law & Ho, 2024, p. 707; Sastry et al., 2024, p. 75), the speed of AI development outstripping institutional response (Hausenloy & Dennis, 2023, pp. 21-22), the prominent role of non-state actors in AI (Hausenloy & Dennis, 2023, p. 22; Zaidi & Dafoe, 2021, p. 5), and the current lack of international consensus on AI risks in contrast to the unified response to the 1945 atomic bombing of Hiroshima (Bodini, 2024, p. 8; Chesterman, 2021, p. 202; A. Wasil, 2024, p. 5)."}]},
  {"question": "How does arXiv 2510.21203 describe the main governance challenges for domestic AI safety regulation, and what sectoral models are referenced?", "answer": "Challenges include establishing risk management, oversight, liability, and learning from incidents; models referenced are nuclear power, aviation, pharmaceuticals, and finance.", "evidence": [{"page": 10, "quote": "Researchers addressing this problem frequently focus on risk management, drawing lessons from the regulation of nuclear power safety and other high-risk sectors such as civil aviation, pharmaceuticals, and finance (Campos et al., 2025; Judge et al., 2025; Manheim et al., 2024; Ortega, 2025; Stein et al., 2024; A. R. Wasil, Clymer, et al., 2024). The core governance challenges motivating these comparisons include establishing effective risk management practices, designing appropriate regulatory oversight structures, assigning liability for potential harms, and creating mechanisms for learning from incidents."}]},
  {"question": "In arXiv 2510.21203, what is the main reason that direct institutional replication from the nuclear domain is considered inappropriate for AI governance?", "answer": "Direct replication is inappropriate due to profound differences: AI's software basis, rapid evolution, and the role of private industry versus state control.", "evidence": [{"page": 11, "quote": "The extensive critiques of an 'IAEA for AI' illustrate this well: scholars consistently highlight how the IAEA's own enforcement challenges, combined with AI's intangible and rapidly evolving nature, make direct institutional replication inappropriate (Cha, 2024; Chesterman, 2021; Law & Ho, 2024)."}]}
]}
